@* The payload of one event, over the table it was listed in. A page of the log is read by running
   down the positions and the types; the payload column opens what a row holds as the properties it
   was read into, and this is the other question about the same row — what the store actually wrote.

   Open because the address says so and closed by a link that takes that back, like the type pop-up
   and every other view on these pages. No script and no <dialog>: these pages render statically,
   the framework's modal needs JavaScript to open at all, and a reader who followed a link here can
   leave the way they came — the close is a link, the backdrop behind it is the same link, and the
   browser's own back button undoes the whole thing. *@
<div class="overlay" id="@Anchor" tabindex="-1">
    @* The way out that a click anywhere outside is. Kept out of the reading order and out of the
       accessibility tree: it is the same address as the Close beside the title, and offered twice
       it would be read out twice. *@
    <a class="overlay-dismiss" href="@CloseLink" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"></a>

    @* Named by its heading rather than marked aria-modal: nothing here traps focus or hides the
       page behind it from a screen reader, and saying otherwise would promise a reader something
       that is not true. What it is instead is a region with a name, which is what it behaves as. *@
    @* Narrower than the sheet the type opens on: that one lays four views out in columns, and this
       one holds a payload, whose lines are short and are read down rather than across. *@
    <section class="dialog dialog-narrow" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="@(Anchor)-title">
        <header class="dialog-header">
            @* The same mark the column that opened this is marked with, so what was clicked and
               what arrived look like the same thing. The type the row was written under and nothing
               else: what kind of thing is open is the title's to say, and which one is said below,
               where a key as long as the sheet is wide has the width to be read. *@
            <h2 id="@(Anchor)-title">
                <JsonMark/>
                @Title
            </h2>

            <a class="dialog-close" href="@CloseLink" aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</a>
        </header>

        <div class="dialog-body">
            @* Which row this is, above the payload it holds: a fact about the row, in the shape the
               facts on a detail page are listed in — the label small and set apart, the value in the
               face every stored value here is set in. *@
            @if (Id is { Length: > 0 } id)
            {
                <p class="dialog-id">
                    <span class="dialog-id-label">@IdLabel</span>
                    <code>@id</code>
                </p>
            }

            <JsonPanel Data="@Data"/>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {

    /// <summary>What the row was written under, as every table here names a type.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the table it was opened from calls the value below: where in the log the row sits, or
    /// the key the store wrote it under.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Given by the caller rather than worked out here, because the two logs name a row by different
    /// things: a DCB event has one position across the whole log, and a streamed event has a
    /// sequence that counts within its stream and so is only unique with the stream in front of it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string IdLabel { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which row it is, or null where there is nothing to say.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Id { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The payload, as the store wrote it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Data { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>This page with nothing asked for, which is what closes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string CloseLink { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>
    /// What this is called in the address, so the link that opens it can land on it.
    /// </summary>
    public const string Anchor = "json";

}
